---
id: org.dxos.review.architecture
name: ArchitectureRules
version: 0.1.0
---

Repo-wide review rules mined from a year of human review comments on
`dxos/dxos` — 348 rule-worthy principles across 158 PRs, clustered and counted
in `.agents/projects/architecture-rules/dataset/CANDIDATES.md`. Each `rule`
block below is discovered by the agentic-review harness, matched against the
files its globs select, and applied by a focused subagent or the System One
checker.

## Extensions

| Term   | URI                   |
| ------ | --------------------- |
| `rule` | org.dxos.mdl.rule@1.0 |

## Rules

```mdl
rule reuse-existing-mechanism: Extend an existing mechanism instead of adding a parallel one
  Before writing a new service, adapter, registry, cache, helper, type, or
  query path, check whether one already covers the concern in this package or
  a sibling package. Flag a second HTTP client for a protocol one client
  already speaks, a second config-building helper, a hand-rolled SQL string
  where the query builder already has the needed clause, or a bespoke
  registry next to the framework's own lifecycle/cleanup mechanism. Do not
  flag a genuinely new capability with no existing analogue, or an existing
  mechanism that is a poor fit on its own merits (wrong data shape, wrong
  lifecycle) — name the concrete gap, don't just assert novelty is bad. Fix:
  delete the new implementation and call the existing one, adding a
  parameter if it needs to vary. Migration/dead-code, internal-vs-public-API,
  and UI-vs-operation duplication are covered by their own, narrower rules
  below — this one is for everything else.
  Source: 85 review comments across 62 PRs, e.g.
  https://github.com/dxos/dxos/pull/10271#discussion_r2597932864.
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/src/**/*.ts
    - packages/**/src/**/*.tsx
  unit: file
  context: diff, similar
  system-one: on
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule delete-dead-code-after-migration: Remove the superseded path in the same change
  When a migration, refactor, or replacement supersedes a mechanism, delete
  the superseded file, type, re-export, or flag in the same change instead of
  leaving it stubbed, unused, or as a compatibility shim. Flag a duplicate
  file left behind after logic moved elsewhere, an unused config escape
  hatch nothing exercises, a deprecated function still called from the new
  code path, or service requirements a refactor no longer needs. Do not flag
  a deprecation window explicitly requested for external/downstream
  consumers — state the exception, don't assume it. Fix: delete the stale
  file/branch/re-export and point every remaining caller at the new location
  in the same PR. `no-compat-shims` in non-negotiables.mdl already covers
  re-export shims left at an old import path; this rule covers the rest of
  what a migration leaves behind — dead branches, unused flags, stale
  service wiring.
  Source: 22 review comments across 21 PRs, e.g.
  https://github.com/dxos/dxos/pull/11796#discussion_r3421630793.
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/*.ts
    - packages/**/*.tsx
  unit: pr
  context: diff, pr
  system-one: off
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule dependency-direction: Lower-level packages never import from higher-level ones
  A package depends on the lowest-level package that provides what it needs.
  A foundational, shared, or protocol package must never import a
  higher-level, UI-specific, or app-specific package or concept just to reuse
  a type or convenience. Flag `@dxos/app-framework` or a common/shared module
  importing a UI library, a protocol/plan/schema package carrying executor or
  UI-only logic, an app-specific helper living in a shared SDK package, or a
  core/compute package importing a UI package merely for a type. Do not flag
  a type-only cross-import (`import type`) between peer API modules —
  reviewers explicitly call these out as not creating the circular-dependency
  problem a value import does. Fix: move the lower-level piece into the
  low-level package, or move the higher-level piece out into the consuming
  layer.
  Source: 23 review comments across 20 PRs, e.g.
  https://github.com/dxos/dxos/pull/10251#discussion_r2585348813.
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/src/**/*.ts
    - packages/**/src/**/*.tsx
  unit: file
  context: diff, package
  system-one: on
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule state-owned-once: Track state on the record that owns it, not in a side-table
  Track a piece of state in exactly one place instead of a parallel
  map/side-table/duplicate field kept in sync by hand; derive rather than
  denormalize. Flag a `WeakMap`/side-table keyed by entity id for data that
  could live on the entity itself, two fields serving the same purpose after
  a rename or migration, a value reconstructed from parts when the producer
  could pass (or the API already derives) the whole thing, or an append-only
  log where only the latest entry is ever read. Do not flag a genuine
  index/cache whose job is a different-shaped lookup over data still owned
  elsewhere — that is a derived read path, not duplication. Fix: move the
  tracked field onto the owning object/record, delete the side-table, and add
  an eviction path if the side-table was serving that role.
  Source: 24 review comments across 18 PRs, e.g.
  https://github.com/dxos/dxos/pull/10913#discussion_r3248967158.
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/src/**/*.ts
    - packages/**/src/**/*.tsx
  unit: file
  context: diff, siblings
  system-one: on
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule no-pointless-indirection: Don't wrap, name, or generalize a value that doesn't need it
  Don't introduce an intermediate variable, wrapper function, explicit
  generic argument, or cache for something already trivial, used once, or
  inferrable — write the direct thing. Flag a className run through a
  class-merging helper with nothing to merge, an explicit `<T>` TypeScript
  already infers, a single-use helper called from exactly one site, a
  memoization cache in front of an already-cheap computation, or a shell
  command wrapped in a script with no added behavior. Do not flag an
  extraction that names a genuinely reused or semantically meaningful chunk,
  even a short one — this is about added value, not line count. Fix: inline
  the value/call at its one use site, or drop the wrapper/cache/explicit type
  argument.
  Source: 20 review comments across 17 PRs, e.g.
  https://github.com/dxos/dxos/pull/13070#discussion_r4002304345.
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/src/**/*.ts
    - packages/**/src/**/*.tsx
  unit: file
  context: diff
  system-one: on
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule no-impossible-state-handling: Fail hard on states the types already exclude
  When a value's absence would already fail an earlier assertion, or a
  context is required for correctness, use `invariant`/throw instead of a
  defensive fallback, optional chaining, or a silently degraded no-op; model
  kind-varying data as a tagged union instead of a struct whose fields only
  apply to some variants. Flag `?? ''`/`??=`/`!` after a preceding assertion
  already guarantees presence, an optional field guarded with `?.`
  everywhere instead of being made required, a struct with fields meaningful
  only for some of its variants, or a branch silently dropped during a
  refactor with no justification. Do not flag a field that is optional
  because the case is a deliberate, documented progressive-enhancement path
  — the file should say so. Fix: add/rely on the `invariant` and delete the
  fallback branch, or make the field required and check it once at the
  boundary.
  Source: 15 review comments across 13 PRs, e.g.
  https://github.com/dxos/dxos/pull/9979#discussion_r2421106448.
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/src/**/*.ts
    - packages/**/src/**/*.tsx
  unit: file
  context: diff
  system-one: on
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule canonical-api-surface: Import the canonical public export, never an internal path
  A package's exports are hidden behind a clear `internal/` boundary;
  application code imports the canonical public type/export, never an
  internal path or a legacy duplicate the public one has superseded. Flag an
  `import` reaching into another package's `internal/` or `src/...` path when
  a public export exists, a public API type still deriving from a legacy
  internal-types module, or a local re-export of something the source
  package already exports directly. Do not flag code inside a package's own
  `internal/` tree importing its own internals — this rule is about crossing
  the package boundary. Fix: switch the import to the public entry point
  (adding the missing export there if needed), and delete the
  internal-reaching import or local re-export.
  Source: 22 review comments across 11 PRs, e.g.
  https://github.com/dxos/dxos/pull/10230#discussion_r2555502347.
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/src/**/*.ts
    - packages/**/src/**/*.tsx
  grep: /internal/|from ['"].*\/src\/
  unit: file
  context: imports, public-api
  system-one: on
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule business-logic-out-of-ui: Sync and integration logic lives in operations, not containers
  Business/integration logic — API calls, sync, data mapping — belongs in an
  Operation or service, independent of UI; a React container calls it rather
  than reimplementing it, and a surface/wiring file stays thin. Flag
  fetch/sync/API-client code written inside a `.tsx` container or inside
  `Surface.create` wiring, the same sync logic implemented once in a
  component and again in an operation, or an AI/model call made with its own
  credentials instead of through the shared AiService/operation. Do not flag
  UI-only state (open/closed, selection, form-local validation) living in the
  component — that is not business logic. Fix: move the logic into an
  Operation/service module and have the component invoke it; move
  component-specific hooks out of the surface-wiring file into the
  container.
  Source: 11 review comments across 11 PRs, e.g.
  https://github.com/dxos/dxos/pull/11018#discussion_r3103360527.
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/plugins/**/src/**/*.tsx
    - packages/apps/**/src/**/*.tsx
  unit: file
  context: diff, siblings
  system-one: on
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule inject-dependencies-via-constructor: Take shared collaborators once, not per-method
  When a module needs several collaborators (a host, an indexer, a query
  engine), take them once through a constructor and store them as fields,
  rather than threading them as a parameter through every method or reaching
  into a global mutable registry. Flag the same dependency passed as an
  argument to every method of a class, a module of loose functions closing
  over an ad hoc context object, or per-object bookkeeping kept in a global
  `Map` instead of on the object. Do not flag a genuinely stateless pure
  function that takes its inputs as parameters — that is correctly not a
  class. Fix: convert the function module into a class (or object) whose
  constructor takes the dependencies, and reference the stored fields from
  its methods.
  Source: 9 review comments across 8 PRs, e.g.
  https://github.com/dxos/dxos/pull/12412#discussion_r3774411485.
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/src/**/*.ts
  unit: file
  context: diff
  system-one: on
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule functions-before-classes: Give behavior a home matching its actual state and lifecycle
  A stateful builder with no real lifecycle should be a plain function;
  conversely, a function that operates on a class's own state should be a
  method on that class, not a free function reaching in from outside. Flag a
  chainable builder class used only to assemble test data with no open/close
  lifecycle, a free function taking an object's internals via an exported
  symbol when it could be a `#private`-backed method, or Effect-based code
  wrapped in a plain class that mixes in Promise glue instead of using
  Effect's own composition. Do not flag a class that has a real open/close
  lifecycle and internal state to protect — that is exactly what classes are
  for here. Fix: replace the lifecycle-less class with a function, or move
  the free function onto the class it operates on as a method with private
  state.
  Source: 5 review comments across 4 PRs, e.g.
  https://github.com/dxos/dxos/pull/10897#discussion_r3030840251.
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/src/**/*.ts
    - packages/**/src/**/*.tsx
  unit: file
  context: diff
  system-one: on
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule fix-root-cause-not-symptom: Diagnose and fix the failure, don't swallow or retry around it
  When something fails unexpectedly, diagnose and fix the actual
  precondition violation, race, or misconfiguration — don't catch-and-swallow
  at the call site, layer another client-side mitigation on an issue already
  diagnosed and fixed upstream, or ship a fix with no known root cause. Flag
  a `catch` that silently discards an error to work around a race instead of
  fixing the race, a new client-side retry/guard added for an issue whose
  upstream fix is already known and pending, or a fix whose description says
  "this should help" without naming a cause. Do not flag a genuine,
  documented defensive boundary at a system edge (a network call) — this
  targets internal invariant violations being masked, not all error
  handling. Fix: remove the workaround, add diagnostics to capture the real
  cause if unknown, and fix the precondition.
  Source: 5 review comments across 4 PRs, e.g.
  https://github.com/dxos/dxos/pull/11332#discussion_r3241126618.
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/src/**/*.ts
    - packages/**/src/**/*.tsx
  unit: file
  context: diff
  system-one: off
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule error-messages-carry-context: An error carries the identifier that lets it be traced
  An error thrown for an unexpected or unsupported case must carry the
  specific id/value encountered and a structured code where one exists, not a
  generic static message, and it should be a typed/domain error rather than a
  raw `Error`. Flag `throw new Error('not found')` with no id interpolated, a
  switch's default branch throwing a message that omits the unexpected value,
  or a failure kind matched on a free-form message string instead of a
  structured code. Do not flag a message that already interpolates the
  relevant value — this is about presence of context, not phrasing. Fix:
  interpolate the id/value into the message, add an error-code field and
  match on that, and extract construction into a small factory if reused.
  Source: 4 review comments across 4 PRs, e.g.
  https://github.com/dxos/dxos/pull/10323#discussion_r2628434855.
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/src/**/*.ts
    - packages/**/src/**/*.tsx
  grep: throw new Error\(
  unit: file
  context: diff
  system-one: on
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule lifecycle-owned-by-its-resource: Create and register cleanup once, in the owning layer
  A resource-scoped job, subscription, or interval must be created once as a
  class-scoped field and opened/closed through that resource's own lifecycle
  hooks, not created ad hoc and left running; runtime-specific behavior
  belongs in the layer that owns that runtime, not a shared orchestrator.
  Flag a `setInterval`/subscription started inline with no destructor
  registered anywhere, a shared host/orchestrator class carrying a constant
  or delay that applies to only one runtime, or all open/close steps
  centralized in one orchestrator instead of each component owning its own.
  Do not flag an orchestrator that legitimately sequences several
  components' own open/close calls — that is coordination, not owning their
  lifecycle. Fix: hoist the construction into a class field and drive it
  from open/close, registering cleanup through the framework's lifecycle
  API; move runtime-specific constants into the runtime-specific module.
  Source: 4 review comments across 4 PRs, e.g.
  https://github.com/dxos/dxos/pull/10539#discussion_r2781711821.
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/src/**/*.ts
  grep: setInterval\(|setTimeout\(|\.subscribe\(
  unit: file
  context: diff, siblings
  system-one: on
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule no-premature-abstraction: Don't add a helper or facade before it has earned its keep
  Don't add a convenience wrapper, facade, or public API surface for a
  pattern that hasn't recurred enough to justify it, and don't reintroduce an
  abstraction — such as dispatch through a mutable handler slot — that was
  deliberately removed for simplicity. Flag a new helper/shorthand added
  speculatively "for later", a facade/adapter that hides a distinction (local
  vs. remote execution) the caller actually needs to branch on correctly, or
  machinery re-added that a prior PR removed on purpose. Do not flag an
  abstraction backed by two or more real call sites today — that has earned
  itself. Fix: remove the speculative API/facade and let callers see the
  real distinction, or wait until a second concrete caller justifies it.
  Source: 4 review comments across 4 PRs, e.g.
  https://github.com/dxos/dxos/pull/10636#discussion_r2863706286.
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/src/**/*.ts
    - packages/**/src/**/*.tsx
  unit: file
  context: diff, importers
  system-one: off
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule dont-recompute-in-reactive-closures: Build expensive resources once, outside recomputation
  Inside a reactive/atom recomputation, don't recreate an expensive resource
  (a query, a `WeakRef` deref) on every run; construct or dereference it once
  outside the closure so its lifetime matches the surrounding scope, and
  cache derived-by-identity values in the shared utility rather than at each
  call site. Flag an `Atom.make` or similar recomputation closure that calls
  a constructor or `.deref()` on every invocation instead of once, or callers
  each adding their own `useMemo` around a pure derivation a shared utility
  should cache itself. Do not flag work that genuinely must re-run because
  its inputs changed. Fix: hoist the construction/deref outside the closure,
  and add cache/eviction (`WeakMap`, `FinalizationRegistry`) to the shared
  utility instead of caller-side memoization.
  Source: 9 review comments across 3 PRs, e.g.
  https://github.com/dxos/dxos/pull/10450#discussion_r2719274285.
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/src/**/*.ts
    - packages/**/src/**/*.tsx
  grep: Atom\.make\(|useMemo\(|\.deref\(\)
  unit: file
  context: diff
  system-one: on
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule structured-logging-not-console: Use the project logger, at the right level, without spam
  Use `@dxos/log`'s structured logger, never `console.log`; mix only one
  logging API per module; and log routine/frequent events below `info` so a
  loop or callback doesn't spam the stream. Flag `console.*` left in
  committed code, a log call inside a loop/callback at `info` or above with
  no throttle, or a file mixing `console` calls and `log` calls. Do not flag
  a genuinely rare, meaningful state transition logged at `info` — this
  targets volume and the wrong API, not logging itself. Fix: replace
  `console.*` with `log`, and drop frequent/internal-detail log calls to
  `debug`/`trace` or gate them.
  Source: 4 review comments across 3 PRs, e.g.
  https://github.com/dxos/dxos/pull/10176#discussion_r2541840597.
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/src/**/*.ts
    - packages/**/src/**/*.tsx
  grep: console\.(log|warn|error|info|debug)\(
  unit: file
  context: diff, siblings
  system-one: on
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule use-context-scoped-cancellation: Schedule timeouts through the ctx-aware scheduler
  Schedule timeouts through the context-aware scheduling helper, not a raw
  `setTimeout`, so they are cancelled automatically on disposal; a caller
  waiting on a scheduled task must still await its actual completion rather
  than returning early on context disposal; and don't wrap a call in an
  external cancellation helper when the callee already accepts and handles a
  context itself. Flag a raw `setTimeout`/`setInterval` with no tie to the
  owning context's disposal, a caller that returns as soon as context is
  disposed while the underlying task keeps running, or a redundant
  cancellation wrapper around an already context-aware call. Do not flag a
  one-shot timer explicitly scoped to process lifetime, not a
  request/operation context. Fix: use the ctx-scoped scheduler helper, keep
  awaiting completion, and remove the redundant wrapper.
  Source: 3 review comments across 3 PRs, e.g.
  https://github.com/dxos/dxos/pull/11332#discussion_r3241046285.
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/src/**/*.ts
  grep: setTimeout\(|setInterval\(
  unit: file
  context: diff
  system-one: on
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule avoid-full-collection-scans: Use the index or an incremental path, not a scan of everything
  Don't scan an entire space/collection or a data structure's full history
  when a targeted, indexed query or a cheap incremental check can answer the
  same question. Flag a "find all objects referencing X" implemented as
  iterate-every-object-in-the-space instead of a reverse-reference index
  lookup, or a value recomputed by scanning from the beginning of history on
  every check instead of from the latest heads. Do not flag a one-time
  migration or admin tool where a full scan is the explicit, acceptable
  cost. Fix: replace the scan with the indexed/targeted query, or compute
  incrementally from the latest state.
  Source: 3 review comments across 3 PRs, e.g.
  https://github.com/dxos/dxos/pull/12412#discussion_r3895355760.
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/src/**/*.ts
  unit: file
  context: diff, similar
  system-one: off
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule scope-multi-tenant-queries-by-space: Every space-scoped query and key leads with spaceId
  A query or index over multi-tenant (multi-space) data must be scoped by
  space id, and that id must be the leading column of any composite key
  meant to be unique or queried within a scope, to avoid leaking or colliding
  across spaces. Flag a query type or SQL statement with no `spaceId` filter
  over data that spans spaces, or a composite index/key whose scope id isn't
  the leading column. Do not flag genuinely space-agnostic system tables
  (account-level metadata) — this applies to per-space data specifically.
  Fix: add the spaceId parameter/filter (joined against the owning table if
  needed) and put the scope id first in the composite key.
  Source: 3 review comments across 2 PRs, e.g.
  https://github.com/dxos/dxos/pull/10388#discussion_r2681040954.
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/src/**/*.ts
  unit: file
  context: diff
  system-one: on
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule construct-populated-dont-mutate-after: Build an object with its data, don't fill it after
  Pass initial data into the constructing call (or a combined
  create-and-insert helper) rather than creating an empty object and
  mutating it with a follow-up call. Flag `Obj.make()` (or similar) followed
  immediately by field assignment or a separate insert call, when a combined
  form exists. Do not flag a builder pattern used for genuinely
  conditional/incremental construction where the fields aren't all known up
  front. Fix: pass the initial values directly into the factory/insert call.
  Source: 2 review comments across 2 PRs, e.g.
  https://github.com/dxos/dxos/pull/9979#discussion_r2421095325.
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/src/**/*.ts
    - packages/**/src/**/*.tsx
  grep: Obj\.make\(
  unit: file
  context: diff
  system-one: on
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule refactor-must-preserve-behavior: A mechanical refactor must not silently change behavior
  A refactor that changes an identifier, representation, or branching
  structure must preserve prior behavior — including the negative/else case
  — as a side effect of the change, not drop it silently. Flag a branch
  rewritten during a refactor where the else-case handling disappears with
  no explanation, or a naming/format change under which prior
  eager-loading (or similar) behavior stops working. Do not flag a
  deliberate behavior change the PR calls out as part of the refactor's
  intent. Fix: restore the dropped branch/behavior, or explicitly justify in
  the PR why it's no longer needed.
  Source: 2 review comments across 2 PRs, e.g.
  https://github.com/dxos/dxos/pull/10421#discussion_r2704439286.
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/src/**/*.ts
    - packages/**/src/**/*.tsx
  unit: pr
  context: diff
  system-one: off
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule batch-queries-not-n-plus-1: One round trip for a batch, not one query per item
  Accept an array/batch parameter and issue a single query — an `IN` clause,
  an upsert — instead of one query per item in a loop, or two round trips
  where one would do. Flag `Promise.all(ids.map(id => query(id)))` where a
  single batched query is possible, or a check-then-write pattern issuing
  two round trips for what an upsert could do in one. Do not flag items that
  genuinely need independent transactions or error isolation. Fix: change
  the method to accept an array and run one query (or upsert) covering all
  of them.
  Source: 2 review comments across 2 PRs, e.g.
  https://github.com/dxos/dxos/pull/10461#discussion_r2730285831.
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/src/**/*.ts
  grep: Promise\.all\(.*\.map\(
  unit: file
  context: diff
  system-one: on
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule namespace-export-with-internal-hiding: A package barrel exports explicitly, never by wildcard
  A package's public surface is an explicit set of named exports from
  `index.ts`, with implementation hidden under `internal/`; a barrel never
  reaches up into a parent directory to import something outside its own
  subtree. This is the package-level counterpart to
  `import-as-namespace-is-all-or-nothing` in code-style.mdl, which covers a
  single namespace module's own directive/filename/re-export agreement —
  this rule is about the barrel's own export shape and reach, not one
  module's internal consistency. Flag `index.ts` doing a blanket wildcard
  re-export of every submodule instead of an explicit public surface, and a
  barrel file importing from `../` outside its own tree. Do not flag a
  `export * as Foo from './Foo'` naming one namespace module explicitly —
  that is the expected shape, not a wildcard. Fix: move internal-only code
  under `internal/`, replace wildcard re-exports with explicit ones, and fix
  upward barrel imports.
  Source: 2 review comments across 2 PRs, e.g.
  https://github.com/dxos/dxos/pull/11729#discussion_r3379156940.
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/src/index.ts
  grep: export \*
  unit: file
  context: siblings, public-api
  system-one: on
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule follow-existing-lazy-loading-pattern: Match the sibling entries' lazy-load wrapper exactly
  When adding an entry to an existing lazily-loaded registry or module list,
  wrap it the same way the sibling entries are wrapped (e.g.
  `Capability.lazy`) instead of importing it eagerly. Flag a new
  capability/module added to a registry with a plain top-level `import`
  while every existing entry in that list uses a lazy wrapper. Do not flag a
  registry that has no established lazy convention yet. Fix: wrap the new
  export in the same lazy-loader factory the existing entries use.
  Source: 2 review comments across 2 PRs, e.g.
  https://github.com/dxos/dxos/pull/11458#discussion_r3314201845.
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/src/**/*.ts
    - packages/**/src/**/*.tsx
  unit: file
  context: siblings
  system-one: on
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule collapse-branches-via-identity-element: Use the type's empty value instead of parallel branches
  When several conditional branches each return a variant of the same
  composition, collapse them into one expression using the type's
  identity/empty element (e.g. `Layer.empty`) instead of branching. Flag
  `if`/`else` chains where each branch differs only in which piece is
  included in an otherwise-identical composition, such as a merge or a layer
  combination. Do not flag branches whose bodies genuinely differ beyond the
  identity substitution. Fix: replace the branches with a single expression
  using a ternary or a fallback to the identity element.
  Source: 2 review comments across 2 PRs, e.g.
  https://github.com/dxos/dxos/pull/12147#discussion_r3553810864.
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/src/**/*.ts
    - packages/**/src/**/*.tsx
  unit: file
  context: diff
  system-one: on
  severity: warn
```

## Appendix

```mdl
ext rule
  uri: org.dxos.mdl.rule@1.0
  desc: A machine-checked review rule applied by an LLM subagent or the System One checker.
  inline-prose: true
  fields:
    files:       Glob | Glob[]     # paths this rule reviews, relative to `scope`
    grep?:       Prose             # optional regex pre-filter over file contents
    severity?:   warn | error      # defaults to warn
    scope?:      dir | repo        # glob base: this file's dir (default) or repo root
    unit?:       file | pr         # one verdict per file (default) or per change set
    context?:    ContextKind[]     # diff | imports | importers | siblings | package | public-api | similar | test | pr
    system-one?: on | off          # off when no fetcher can supply what the rule needs
    question?:   Prose             # literal yes/no question for the System One checker
```
